We consider an \(8 \times 8\) grid, with Jacob and Bojac starting at possibly different cells. They listen to the same sequence of cardinal directions. Jacob moves one cell in the called direction if possible; otherwise, he stays. Bojac moves one cell in the direction \(90^\circ\) counterclockwise from the called direction if possible; otherwise, he stays. We want the maximum number of distinct ordered pairs (Jacob's position, Bojac's position) reachable by some choice of starting positions and some sequence of instructions.

By choosing starting positions appropriately and exploiting boundary conditions to decouple their motions, it is possible to achieve any pair of positions for Jacob and Bojac. For example, starting with Jacob at \((1,1)\) and Bojac at \((8,8)\), one can design sequences to reach any target pair \((J, B)\). Since there are \(8 \times 8 = 64\) possible positions for each person, the total number of ordered pairs is \(64 \times 64 = 4096\). Thus, the maximum possible number of distinct reachable pairs is \(4096\).

\boxed{4096}
